          specifically for serious hill-training runners. The Sole F80 is the
          closest non-NordicTrack rival at a similar price, trading the iFit
          ecosystem for a subscription-free console — worth considering if
          you already know you don't want a coaching app tied to your
          treadmill.
        </p>
        <p>
          One more comparison worth making mentally, even though it isn't in
          the table above: NordicTrack's own entry-level T Series machines,
          which typically run $600-$900. The gap in raw specs looks smaller
          on paper than it feels in practice — the Commercial 1750's frame
          is noticeably more rigid under load, the deck cushioning is more
          adjustable, and the touchscreen is both larger and more responsive.
          If your budget genuinely can't stretch to Commercial-tier pricing,
          a T Series machine is a reasonable fallback, but understand you're
          trading away some of the durability and comfort headroom that
          makes the 1750 worth recommending in the first place.
