        <p>
          As much as we like the Commercial 1750, it isn't the right machine
          for every shopper who lands on this page. If you live in an
          apartment and need the treadmill to genuinely disappear between
          uses, its large folded footprint will frustrate you more than it
          helps — a dedicated compact folding treadmill from our
          <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews">folding treadmill guide</a>
          will serve you far better. If you know for certain you'll never pay
          for a subscription app, you're paying for touchscreen hardware
          you'll rarely use at full value, and a simpler console-based
          machine at a lower price point may be the smarter buy. And if your
          household's use case is genuinely light — occasional walking a
          couple times a month rather than a real fitness habit — this is
          more treadmill than you need, and the money is better spent on a
          machine from our
          <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews">budget treadmill guide</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">Final Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          The NordicTrack Commercial 1750 earns its place as one of the
          better mid-range treadmills we've tested, specifically because it
          doesn't cut corners on the two things that matter most day to day:
          motor strength and deck comfort. The iFit dependency is a real
          trade-off, not a minor footnote, and you should go in with eyes
          open about the ongoing subscription cost if you want the full
          experience. But judged purely as a piece of exercise equipment —
          motor, deck, frame, cushioning — this is a treadmill built to be
          used hard for years, not a treadmill built to look good in a
          product photo.
        </p>
