              that feel flimsy at running speeds and flagship models like the
              Commercial 2450 that cost hundreds more for incremental gains
              most walkers and joggers won't notice.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I have to pay for iFit to use the Commercial 1750?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              The treadmill functions without an active iFit subscription,
              but most of its standout features — auto-adjusting incline
              during trainer-led workouts, the full workout library, and the
              interactive touchscreen content — require a membership. Without
              it, you're left with a manual treadmill on a premium-priced
              frame, so budget for the subscription as part of the real cost.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does the Commercial 1750 compare to the Commercial 2450?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              The Commercial 2450 has a stronger 4.25 CHP motor and steeper
              15% max incline versus the 1750's 3.75 CHP and 12% incline,
              along with a larger touchscreen. For most walkers and moderate
              runners, the 1750 delivers nearly the same day-to-day
              experience for several hundred dollars less; the 2450 earns its
              price for serious runners who use steep incline training
              regularly.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the deck big enough for taller runners?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              The 22 x 60-inch deck is on the larger end for a home
              treadmill and comfortably fit testers up to 6'3" during
              full-stride running without feeling cramped. Shorter or
              narrower decks on cheaper machines are the ones where taller
              runners typically run into trouble, not this one.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How loud is the Commercial 1750 at higher speeds?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              We measured noise in the low-60 decibel range during sustained
              jogging, roughly comparable to a normal conversation. It's not
              silent, but it didn't interfere with phone calls in an
              adjacent room during our testing, and it stayed consistent
              rather than getting noticeably louder over several weeks of
              daily use.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does assembly take?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Roughly 75 minutes working alone, or about 45 minutes with a
              second person helping lift the upright frame into place, which
              the manual specifically recommends for that step. The console
              and mast arrive mostly pre-wired, which speeds things up
              compared to machines that require extensive cable routing
              during assembly.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What maintenance does the Commercial 1750 need over time?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Belt tracking and tension are the most common maintenance
              items owners report needing within the first year, which is
              normal for treadmills generally rather than specific to this
              model. Keeping the belt lubricated on NordicTrack's suggested
              schedule is the single biggest factor in avoiding motor strain
              and premature wear.
            </p>
